// ─── headscale ACL policy — the fleet's tailnet, and the friend fleet ──────
//
// Loaded by hosts/nas/headscale.nix as services.headscale.settings.policy.path
// (mode = "file"). It rides into /nix/store on every rebuild, so this file IS
// the policy: there is no web console, no database copy, and no way to change
// the ACL without a diff. Edits apply with `systemctl reload headscale`
// (SIGHUP) — no restart, no node churn.
//
// huJSON: comments and trailing commas are legal here. That is the whole
// reason upstream chose the format, and this file leans on it hard.
//
// ── TODAY'S CONTENT IS ALLOW-ALL, DELIBERATELY ────────────────────────────
// The only node on this tailnet today is the NAS itself. What matters on day
// one is not the rules, it is that the MECHANISM exists before the first
// friend key is ever minted: policy in git, reviewed in a diff, rebuilt onto
// the box, reloadable. Retrofitting that later — after a friend's phone is
// already registered — is how a sandbox gets skipped.
//
// It is also deliberately the SIMPLEST policy that names no user and no tag.
// A policy referencing `tom@` or `tag:mesh` before those exist can fail
// validation, and headscale refuses to serve on a policy it cannot load; on
// the box that is the house router, a service that will not start because of
// an ACL for devices that do not exist yet is a self-inflicted outage. Every
// name below therefore appears only inside a comment until the thing it names
// is real.
//
// 2026-09-01, LEARNED ON THE FIRST DEPLOY, THE EXPENSIVE WAY: this file
// originally used a `grants` block, on the research claim that upstream calls
// `acls` legacy and `grants` current. The LIVE 0.29.3 binary on this box
// refused it — `parsing policy: unknown field "grants"` — which crash-looped
// headscale, timed out the enroll unit, and failed the whole NAS activation
// (deploy-rs rolled the box back; nothing was lost). `grants` is
// tailscale.com's newer syntax; the headscale actually packaged here speaks
// `acls`. So: `acls` in this file, everywhere, until a headscale release
// whose OWN release notes say otherwise — and the guaranteed-valid floor
// remains the empty policy `{}` (allow-all, nothing to parse).
//
// ─── THE SCHEME THIS GROWS INTO ───────────────────────────────────────────
// Written now, in the file it will land in, so the future public
// `omarchy-nix-fleet` repo (agenix-encrypted preauth keys, one per friend
// device) has an unambiguous target to mint against and needs ZERO
// headscale-side redesign when it appears. Full narrative, plus the
// key-minting runbook, in docs/nas/headscale-2026-09-01.md.
//
// USERS: one per PERSON, never per device.
//     headscale users create tom
//     headscale users create <friend>
//   A person's laptop and phone group naturally under their own user, which is
//   what makes `<friend>@` a meaningful ACL subject and what makes
//   `autogroup:self` useful. One user per device would make every grant a
//   hand-maintained list.
//
// TAGS: tag Tom's INFRASTRUCTURE, never a friend's personal device.
//     tag:mesh    — Tom's NixOS fleet nodes (continuity: modules/secrets.nix
//                   already records tag:mesh as the scheme the retired
//                   tailscale.com authkeys used, so this is the same name
//                   carried across the control-plane move, not a new idea)
//     tag:shared  — the one or two things friends are actually invited to
//   Friends' phones/laptops stay UNTAGGED personal devices under their own
//   user. That is what keeps `autogroup:member` and `<friend>@` honest as ACL
//   subjects, and it is also the polite arrangement: a tag is an assertion
//   about a machine Tom administers.
//
// THE DENY-ALL BASELINE, to paste in when the first friend user exists. Note
// that `acls` must be explicit from then on — omitting it means allow-all,
// which is fine for a one-node tailnet and wrong the moment it is not.
// (`acls` syntax, NOT `grants` — see the 2026-09-01 note above; ports ride in
// the dst string as `target:port`.)
//
//   "tagOwners": {
//     "tag:mesh":   ["tom@"],
//     "tag:shared": ["tom@"],
//   },
//   "acls": [
//     // Tom reaches everything.
//     { "action": "accept", "src": ["tom@"],      "dst": ["*:*"] },
//     // The fleet reaches the fleet.
//     { "action": "accept", "src": ["tag:mesh"],  "dst": ["tag:mesh:*"] },
//     // Each friend gets ONE narrow, explicitly-ported grant to the shared
//     // surface and nothing else. Friends never see each other and never see
//     // Tom's fleet, because nothing above grants it.
//     { "action": "accept", "src": ["<friend>@"], "dst": ["tag:shared:443"] },
//   ],
//   "ssh": [
//     // Tailscale SSH stays inside Tom's own devices. modules/common.nix puts
//     // --ssh on every node's up/set flags; without a rule here that flag
//     // grants nothing, which is the correct default for a tailnet that has
//     // guests on it.
//     { "action": "accept", "src": ["tom@"], "dst": ["tag:mesh"],
//       "users": ["autogroup:nonroot", "tom"] },
//   ],
//   // Route approval for the house LAN, so the NAS's --advertise-routes stops
//   // needing a manual `headscale nodes approve-routes`. Depends on the tag
//   // being owned, which is why it lands with this block and not before it.
//   "autoApprovers": {
//     "routes": { "10.42.0.0/24": ["tag:mesh"] },
//   },
//
// AUTOGROUPS this headscale (0.28.0, nixpkgs-stable 26.05 — NOT 0.29.3 as first written; verified `headscale version` 2026-09-01) supports:
//   autogroup:internet (dst only), autogroup:member, autogroup:tagged,
//   autogroup:self (dst only), autogroup:nonroot (ssh `users` only),
//   autogroup:danger-all (src only).
// NOT supported, so do not reach for them: autogroup:admin, device-posture
// conditions, IP sets — and `grants` (refused live 2026-09-01, note above).
//
// VERIFY BEFORE TRUSTING: `tagOwners` parsing at this exact version was not
// confirmed from source, only from the shared Tailscale policy schema. When
// the block above is first pasted in, deploy it and check
// `journalctl -u headscale` for a policy-load error BEFORE minting a friend
// key against it. The server-side `headscale nodes tag -i <id> -t tag:mesh`
// path works regardless and needs no tagOwners at all — that is the fallback.
{
  "acls": [
    // src "*" = everyone, dst "*:*" = everything on all ports. Equivalent to
    // omitting the section, but written out so the shape is visible and the
    // deny-all switch above is a paste rather than an act of authorship.
    // This exact rule is upstream's own documented allow-all — and unlike its
    // `grants` predecessor, it has now been parsed by the binary that counts.
    { "action": "accept", "src": ["*"], "dst": ["*:*"] },
  ],
}
